{"acceptance_length": 2.7866666666666666, "acceptance_lengths": [1, 1, 2, 4, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 3, 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 5, 3, 3, 1, 1, 1, 1, 8, 8, 3, 8, 2, 3, 2, 4, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 2, 1, 5, 2, 1, 4, 1, 2, 2, 8, 2, 4, 8, 5, 5, 7, 8, 8, 8, 7, 5, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2], "accepted_draft_lengths": [0, 0, 1, 3, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 2, 2, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 4, 2, 2, 0, 0, 0, 0, 7, 7, 2, 7, 1, 2, 1, 3, 0, 1, 1, 2, 0, 1, 0, 4, 1, 0, 3, 0, 1, 1, 7, 1, 3, 7, 4, 4, 6, 7, 7, 7, 6, 4, 3, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 21574759424, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 21574753792, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "You can write a Python function to check if there is any **common element** between two sequences using Python's built-in `set` data structure. Here's a simple and efficient implementation:\n\n```python\ndef overlapping(seq1, seq2):\n    return bool(set(seq1) & set(seq2))\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- `set(seq1)` and `set(seq2)` convert the sequences into sets.\n- The `&` operator finds the intersection of the two sets.\n- `bool(...)` converts the result to a boolean: `True` if there is at least one common element, `False` otherwise.\n\n### Test Cases:\n```python\nassert overlapping([1,2,3,4,5], [6,7,8,9]) == False\nassert overlapping([1,2,3], [4,5,6]) == False\nassert overlapping([1,4,5], [1,4,5]) == True\n```\n\nAll of these assertions will pass with this implementation.", "decode_seconds": 3.6758331060409546, "draft_calls": 75, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 129, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "a622089f43bc30ac54384dd578a9d92a2045c30af79a69ddb29e2a8e1b92bd8d", "method": "relay_last2", "output_hash": "8b447edcb3fbfb5b43b46e24e15d0a1c51e6113e51116e57ee8116437272e634", "output_tokens": 210, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 21705633280, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 21906849792, "problem_id": "Mbpp/414", "profile_regions_ms": {"draft": 1288.3597124814987, "prefill_full_target": 34.02297592163086, "prefill